*Key Physical Findings:*
1. **Pre-Peak Convergence:** In the pre-peak regime ($u \le 0.0055\,\mathrm{mm}$), external work agrees across all 12 models within $0.15\%$ ($0.002035$ to $0.002038\,\mathrm{kN\cdot mm}$) and reaction force agrees within $0.27\%$ ($0.7187$ to $0.7207\,\mathrm{kN}$).
2. **Early Softening in Finer Meshes:** At $u=0.0057\,\mathrm{mm}$, finer meshes have initiated softening ($S_5$ has peaked at $u=5.575\,\mu\mathrm{m}$, $S_4$ at $5.606\,\mu\mathrm{m}$, $S_3$ at $5.633\,\mu\mathrm{m}$, $S_2$ at $5.711\,\mu\mathrm{m}$), demonstrating that fine mesh resolution accelerates crack initiation.
3. **A4 Work Inflation Mechanism:** For case $A_4$, pre-peak work at $u = 5.50\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ is $0.002038\,\mathrm{kN\cdot mm}$ (matching $S_1$ within $0.01\%$). However, because the $5\%$ error target generates only $4{,}194$ elements ($h \gg l_0$), the coarse background mesh locks the localized damage zone (numerical crack-pinning), producing an artificial plateau ($F \approx 0.75\,\mathrm{kN}$) through $u = 8.0\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ and inflating terminal work to $0.0042646\,\mathrm{kN\cdot mm}$ ($+80.7\%$ over $S_1$).

